  geopolitics: "The oil transmission has split into two grades and that split is the most useful thing in this section today. WTI is 98.797, back under the hundred handle, up 0.26% on the day and 4.95% on the week; Brent is 109.878, up 0.30% on the day and 9.51% on the month, which has widened the spread to 11.08. The reason is that the two prices are now answering different questions. Brent prices Hormuz, and Hormuz has not improved: Iran continues to say the strait is closed against US statements that commercial vessels are still moving, UKMTO logged ten shipping attacks in the week to Friday, and a laden supertanker carrying more than two million barrels with its tracking systems switched off struck a mine and caught fire, with no independent confirmation of the cause. The Houthis are separately blockading Bab el-Mandeb to Saudi-flagged tankers, so both ends of the Red Sea route are compromised. The seven-day proposal remains the only live diplomatic structure - the strait reopens on day six if the US lifts its blockade of Iranian ports, with negotiations starting on day seven - and Iran is still reviewing it while its chief negotiator says the strait will not reopen until Tehran's demands are met; Trump has announced a crushing economic operation with talks in limbo, and Qatar, mediating, has said an Iran-Oman agreement on future control of the strait would help restart US-Iran talks. WTI, by contrast, is now pricing American policy: Trump's executive order waived the Graham Act sanctions and tariffs on buyers of Russian crude two days before they were due to take effect, with a separate Treasury waiver letting Russian diesel sales run to April 2027, and that global supply offset is what pulled the US grade under 100 while the seaborne grade held 110. The FX read follows directly. For CAD the oil leg is supportive but it is no longer the main engine - the Bank of Canada repricing to 75% for October 28 is, and the Business Outlook Survey showed firms passing on war and tariff costs, which is the energy shock arriving through expectations rather than through the oil price itself. That makes the CAD long more robust than a pure oil trade and it is why USD/CAD and EUR/CAD carry the file's only HIGH convictions. For the euro the energy channel is a straight terms-of-trade tax: EU gas is 88.31 EUR/MWh and German gas 89.29, up 20.56% and 20.48% on the month and up 213.58% and 197.61% on the year, which taxes the bloc's industrial base, feeds the GER40 short through input costs, and gives the ECB an overshoot it has said it will look through. For the yen the energy import bill is the chronic weight but the acute constraint is the Ministry of Finance, not crude. And for the metals the geopolitical premium has simply stopped working: gold is minus 4.60% on the month and silver minus 12.98% despite all of the above, because the ten-year TIPS yield falling from 2.91% to 2.74% between October 9 and 16 produced no rally, and this week's actual haven flow went into the Swiss franc and Treasuries instead. The second geopolitical theatre is new and it is European rather than Middle Eastern: weekend cost-of-living protests across France and Italy escalated into clashes with security forces and more than two thousand arrests in France, and the bond market repriced it as sovereign credit rather than as news. The OAT rose 14.5 basis points to 4.931% and now trades 36 basis points through the Italian BTP at 4.571%, OAT-Bund reached 147.8 basis points at around a two-year wide, and critically the Bund rose 4.2 basis points while Treasuries fell 1.4 and gilts fell 1.0 - the euro area repricing itself, not global duration. Banks led the equity losses, which is the sovereign-bank feedback channel the textbook warns about, with BNP Paribas, Societe Generale and Credit Agricole all down more than 2%. The French Budget sits before the National Assembly and both countries face elections next year. This is the single biggest swing factor on the board: if it calms, EUR goes back to zero, both European index shorts come off and the EUR/CHF trade loses its engine. Not financial advice.",
